---------------------------------- Commentary ----------------------------------

This repository holds two different kinds of thing, and they want two
different licenses.

The labs are meant to be run, forked, pasted into your own harness, and
argued with. A book that tells you to check its numbers and then makes
the checking code awkward to reuse would be a bad joke. So everything
executable here is MIT, which is about as close to "help yourself" as a
license gets.

The prose is different. The chapters are written in a particular voice,
and the site's look is part of what the book is. I would rather not
find a near-copy of it online with the sentences shuffled, or a print
edition of it for sale that I had nothing to do with and cannot vouch
for. So the writing and the design are under a Creative Commons license
that allows sharing with attribution but not commercial use and not
derivative versions.

None of this is meant to stop you learning from it, quoting it, linking
to it, or telling people they are wrong about fsync using arguments you
found here. Do all of that.

